Recommended Pattern for GAS-Compatible TypeScript Development
Author all your .ts files using TypeScript modules
Use export and import for classes, functions, and types.
This gives you full editor support, type safety, and modularity.
Use a TypeScript config for GAS builds:
Set target: "ES5" (for maximum GAS compatibility).
Set module: "ESNext" (so you can use modern module syntax).
Example:
{
  "compilerOptions": {
    "target": "ES5",
    "module": "ESNext",
    // ...other options...
  }
}
Bundle with Webpack and attach to the global namespace:
In your Webpack config, use:
output: {
  library: { type: 'assign', name: 'globalThis.CAMPAIGN' },
  // ...other options...
}
This bundles all modules and attaches them to globalThis.CAMPAIGN, making them accessible in the GAS runtime.
<hr></hr>
Benefits of This Pattern
No need for post-processing scripts like demodulify_for_gas.js.
Consistent authoring experience: All code uses modules.
GAS compatibility: Final output is plain JS, with all exports attached to the global namespace.
Editor and tooling support: TypeScript modules enable navigation, refactoring, and type checking.




